WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.924
Pulse compression turns a long chirp into a sharp peak.

2
00:00:03.974 --> 00:00:13.027
A plain linear chirp, though, leaves sidelobes only about 13 decibels down, enough to hide a small target next to a large one.

3
00:00:13.677 --> 00:00:17.063
The usual fix is a window on the receive filter.

4
00:00:17.113 --> 00:00:26.010
Sidelobes drop a long way, but the filter no longer matches the pulse, and you give up about 1.4 decibels of signal to noise.

5
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.038
Nonlinear FM tapers the transmitted spectrum instead.

6
00:00:30.088 --> 00:00:38.642
The sweep slows down near the centre frequency and speeds up at the edges, so the pulse spends more energy in the middle of the band.

7
00:00:39.292 --> 00:00:41.866
The matched filter is still matched.

8
00:00:41.916 --> 00:00:45.948
Sidelobes fall to about 33 decibels with no loss at all.

9
00:00:45.998 --> 00:00:52.509
The price is Doppler tolerance: a fast mover degrades this pulse more than a linear chirp.
